## Is epilepsy a disability?

Under the **Equality Act 2010**, a condition counts as a disability if it has a **substantial** (more than minor) and **long-term** (12 months or more) effect on your normal daily activities. The law looks at how you would be **without treatment**. Because the law looks at you without medicine, most people with epilepsy are covered, even if their seizures are controlled.

## At work

An employer should look at your individual situation, not make assumptions. Reasonable adjustments could include:

- a **risk assessment** and a seizure plan
- avoiding work at heights or with dangerous machinery, if needed
- avoiding shift patterns that disrupt sleep
- time to recover after a seizure
- changes to duties if you cannot drive

**Access to Work** can help pay for taxis to work if you cannot drive.

## Benefits

PIP is based on how epilepsy affects you on most days. Think about the risk of harm if you had a seizure. It can affect activities such as:

- **preparing food**, if you need supervision to use a cooker safely
- **washing and bathing**, if you need someone nearby
- **managing treatments**, if you need help or reminders
- **planning and following journeys**, if you need someone with you to stay safe

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

## If you cannot work

If your health means you cannot work, or can only work a little, you may be able to get [New Style Employment and Support Allowance](/benefits/esa/) or extra money in [Universal Credit](/benefits/universal-credit-health/). These are separate from PIP, and you can get them at the same time.

## Other help

- **free prescriptions** in England with a medical exemption certificate
- a [Disabled Persons Railcard](/support/travel/disabled-persons-railcard/) if you have repeated attacks or are not allowed to drive
- you may get a free [bus pass](/support/travel/bus-passes/) if you are refused a driving licence

Free, independent advisers, such as **Citizens Advice** or a local welfare rights service, can help you check what you can claim and fill in forms.
